{"type":"message","id":"f79bf090","parentId":"32cc5862","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:15.315Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7MobFRcJL2vLOT8zPfcfj8Y2","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/coindesk.md","limit":220}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_EnWrEwN33Iaxix3Tqfen9Bct","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/cointelegraph.md","limit":220}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_mAx7ENQIaQIM472hbTC4yD4G","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/decrypt.md","limit":220}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":502,"output":122,"cacheRead":21888,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":22512,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779523453243,"responseId":"chatcmpl-Dibd9SUF2wfkBp2Ys8nKfVYlubbl1"}}
{"type":"message","id":"ca59ae73","parentId":"f79bf090","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:15.349Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7MobFRcJL2vLOT8zPfcfj8Y2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Congress hits Polymarket and Kalshi with a massive insider trading probe\n\n## Rep. James Comer is demanding internal records from the prediction market CEOs, warning that government employees could be using classified information to make “huge profits”.\n\n#### What to know:\n\n- The House Oversight Committee has opened an investigation into major prediction markets Polymarket and Kalshi over concerns that U.S. government employees may be using insider information to profit from policy and national security events.\n- Chair James Comer is seeking internal records from the platforms’ CEOs and has signaled he may pursue legislation to bar members of Congress, administration officials and other government employees from participating in prediction markets.\n- The probe comes amid rapid growth in prediction markets and mounting bipartisan scrutiny in Congress, with lawmakers and experts warning of national security risks, potential insider trading and problem gambling tied to the booming industry.\n\nThe U.S. House Oversight Committee plans a probe into the largest prediction market platforms over suspicions that government employees could be exploiting classified information for personal gain.\n\nRep. James Comer, R-Ky., chair of the House Oversight and Government Reform Committee, is seeding the internal records from the CEOs of Polymarket and Kalshi to determine if government employees are using insider knowledge to profit from policy and geopolitical and military operations, he said on CNBC's Squawk Box on Friday.\n\n“There’s a concern now that members of Congress, members of the president’s administration, any type of government employee, can use basic insider knowledge and make huge profits on anything government-related,” Comer told CNBC.\n\n“So we want to not only launch an investigation to see how widespread this has been thus far, but also to prove a case that we’ve got to pass some type of legislation,” Comer added. “And I think it wouldn’t be too much to ask to say members of Congress can’t participate in the predictions market, nor can government employees or people in the president’s administration.”\n\nComer’s probe is the most recent in a series of attempts by Congress to investigate prediction markets and bring insider trading under control.\n\nIn letters sent Friday to Polymarket’s Shayne Coplan and Kalshi’s Tarek Mansour, Comer demanded clarity on how the platforms handle identity verification, enforce geographic restrictions and flag anomalous trading activity.\n\nPrediction markets, which surged in popularity in recent years, have drawn scrutiny from federal and state lawmakers and regulators, who worry the platforms are ripe for exploitation by bad actors with national security clearances.\n\nPrediction market volumes could peak to roughly $1 trillion by 2030, as the sector evolves from niche wagering into broad-based “information markets” spanning sports, crypto, politics and the economy, according to a Wall Street broker Bernstein report in April. Volumes hit $51 billion last year and could reach about $240 billion in 2026.\n\nThe House probe follows a heated U.S. Senate Commerce Committee hearing on Wednesday, where lawmakers from both parties heavily scrutinized prediction market platforms like Kalshi and Crypto.com. Senate Commerce Chair Ted Cruz (R-Texas) blasted the industry for enabling cheating scandals across major sports leagues, warning that the opportunity to profit on event contracts tempts athletes and officials to manipulate outcomes. Meanwhile, Senator John Hickenlooper (D-Colo.) accused the firms' aggressive social media marketing of \"preying on our young people\" and fostering problem gambling.\n\nNicolas Vaiman, co-founder and CEO of onchain intelligence layer Bubblemaps, expressed deep concern over the national security implications of a new wave of insider trading in an interview with CoinDesk.\n\nHe warned that if those observing the predictions markets can spot irregular trades, so can enemies of the United States. He and his team found 80 bets on Polymarket with a 98% win rate, which he said is statistically impossible to achieve. “Not even luck can explain those wins.”\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523455349}}
{"type":"message","id":"9723b8d4","parentId":"ca59ae73","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:15.350Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_EnWrEwN33Iaxix3Tqfen9Bct","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"*Source: **James Comer*\n\nThe “suspiciously timed trades” to which Comer was referring included those from a May 13 New York Times report, detailing incidents of prediction market users betting on Israel’s military actions against Iran, US President Donald Trump announcing a ceasefire in the country’s war with Iran and event contracts related to congressional elections.\n\nPolymarket said in March that it had updated its approach to potential insider trading on the platform, while Kalshi announced in April that it had banned three US politicians for betting on their own races.\n\n**Related: ****Polymarket team says user funds safe as exploit losses climb above $600K**\n\nA Polymarket spokesperson said that the platform “maintains a comprehensive market integrity framework” and it would engage with Comer and the committee on the company’s transparency. A spokesperson for Kalshi said that the company was “proud of [its] comprehensive protections against insider trading” and would likewise engage with US lawmakers.\n\nIn April, the US Justice Department announced a criminal indictment against Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke, a soldier who was involved in the military operation that led to the capture of Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro. Prosecutors alleged that Van Dyke used event contracts on Polymarket related to Maduro’s capture to profit by more than $400,000 using classified information.\n\nVan Dyke pleaded not guilty to the charges, which included commodities fraud and the unlawful use of confidential government information for personal gain. He was released on $250,000 bail and limited to traveling between areas of North Carolina, California and New York.\n\n**Magazine: ****ETH bears growling, Tom Lee’s buying, XRP to ‘explode’: Market Moves**\n\nMore on the subject\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523455349}}
{"type":"message","id":"4590eef5","parentId":"9723b8d4","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:15.350Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_mAx7ENQIaQIM472hbTC4yD4G","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#### In brief\n\n- Kalshi has unveiled Americans for Fair Markets (AFM), a \"well-capitalized\" advocacy group designed to lobby Capitol Hill.\n- AFM has tapped Taylor Budowich, a prominent former White House communications staffer and pro-Trump Super PAC leader.\n- The group's debut comes immediately after House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer (R-KY) launched insider trading investigations.\n\nKalshi unveiled an advocacy group on Friday to help shape policymakers’ perception of prediction markets, billing the move as a way to level the playing field against sportsbooks and casinos.\n\nAmericans for Fair Markets (AFM) will actively combat “interests that are focused on protecting their monopolies and seeding lies,” Kalshi said in a blog post, highlighting the organization’s focus on legislation that supports innovation, market integrity, and consumer protection.\n\nKalshi said the group has tapped Taylor Budowich, a former senior White House staffer who resigned in September, to serve as a strategic advisor. The longtime aide previously helped lead communications for the White House and a pro-Trump super PAC.\n\nThe organization's website borrows its color scheme from Kalshi’s logo, but Americans for Fair Markets has other members, a spokesperson told *Decrypt*. They declined to say how much funding the organization has received, but they described the group as “well capitalized.”\n\nAmericans for Fair Markets’ debut underscores Kalshi’s high-stakes efforts to differentiate itself from chief rival Polymarket and traditional betting platforms on Capitol Hill as prediction markets face intensifying scrutiny from lawmakers over their potential to enrich insiders.\n\nOn Friday, for example, House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer (R-KY) announced investigations into insider trading on Polymarket and Kalshi. The lawmaker referenced suspicious bets on military actions tied to Venezuela and Iran, which have motivated arrests in the U.S. and Israel in recent months.\n\nAlthough both platforms have recently touted beefed-up safeguards, Kalshi has been vocal about its status as a federally regulated exchange under the CFTC. Polymarket has a regulated platform in the U.S. as well, but a majority of its bets are made on its international counterpart.\n\n“A handful of legislators and a gaming lobby want to ban prediction markets outright,” AFM’s website says. “It would push them offshore—onto unregulated platforms with no identity verification, no consumer protections, no insider trading rules, and no cop on the beat.”\n\nBut the gaming lobby claims it’s the prediction platforms that are actually doing the deceiving, a stance American Gaming Association President and CEO Bill Miller made clear during testimony before Congress on Wednesday.\n\n“These so-called prediction markets are deceptively calling sports betting financial contracts and investing,\" he said. “Despite messaging designed to beguile policymakers and the public, they are increasingly being exposed as backdoor sports-betting operations.”\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523455350}}
{"type":"message","id":"708d1739","parentId":"4590eef5","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:20.533Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_Gx7dMF4ZA6Hf2SpymeDwlH9J","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-093013/research/raw.json","limit":220}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bLVIgF9VuitBHhUwL4dejYqf","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-101746/research/raw.json","limit":220}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_r8YMCULgC5ZNSs24viTNcw9B","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-083045/research/raw.json","limit":220}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":2052,"output":365,"cacheRead":22400,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":24817,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779523455351,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DibdBuUzH2vEu2qvWAW039dgZtLSf"}}
{"type":"message","id":"7481588e","parentId":"708d1739","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:20.549Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_Gx7dMF4ZA6Hf2SpymeDwlH9J","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"polymarket-japan-approval-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Polymarket's push for prediction-market approval in Japan by 2030\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Polymarket Japan\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Polymarket targets Japan approval by 2030 as the prediction-market platform expands beyond hostile jurisdictions\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\",\n    \"Crypto Briefing\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/polymarket-aims-for-prediction-market-approval-in-japan-by-2030\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-japan-approval-2030/\",\n    \"https://cryptobriefing.com/polymarket-japan-prediction-market-approval/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinDesk reported that Polymarket has appointed Mike Eidlin, head of Japan at crypto exchange Jupiter, to lead an effort to win authorization for prediction markets in Japan by 2030.\",\n    \"Both CoinDesk and BeInCrypto said the plan is being reported against a Bloomberg backdrop and is motivated by Polymarket's need to expand into major markets as legal pressure and access restrictions complicate its business elsewhere.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto said Japan is currently on Polymarket's frontend-restricted list, and framed the country as a large untapped market despite strict gambling laws and a cautious stance toward crypto businesses.\",\n    \"Crypto Briefing added that Polymarket is trying to reframe prediction markets to regulators as information-aggregation tools rather than simple gambling products, which is likely central to any Japanese approval path.\",\n    \"The reported timeline runs to 2030, implying a long lobbying and regulatory process rather than an imminent launch; Japan's legal treatment of event contracts remains unsettled and could determine whether the market is viewed as gambling, a financial instrument, or a new category.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto noted that Polymarket had previously been blocked in the United States for roughly three years before regaining CFTC clearance in September 2025, underscoring that the company is now pursuing legitimacy through formal market entry rather than gray-zone access.\",\n    \"Crypto Briefing said a successful Japanese authorization would be a major precedent for prediction markets in a G7 country with restrictive gambling laws and could influence nearby Asian jurisdictions with similarly cautious regulatory frameworks.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/polymarket-aims-for-prediction-market-approval-in-japan-by-2030\\n\\n# Polymarket aims for prediction market approval in Japan by 2030\\n\\nCoinDesk reported that Polymarket is set to lobby for authorization of prediction markets in Japan and is aiming for government approval by 2030. The article said the decentralized prediction-market platform has appointed a representative in the country and identified Mike Eidlin, head of Japan at crypto exchange Jupiter, as the person leading the effort. CoinDesk described the move as part of a broader need for Polymarket to expand into major markets as legal scrutiny has hampered its activity in the United States. The piece also emphasized that Japan has some of the strictest gambling laws in the world, with most forms of betting prohibited under the criminal code, apart from state-sanctioned exceptions such as horse racing and lotteries. It added that Japan has also taken a cautious approach to crypto-related businesses through licensing and consumer-protection rules. CoinDesk included a spokesperson comment saying Polymarket has seen meaningful organic interest from users in Japan.\\n\\nSOURCE: BeInCrypto\\nURL: https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-japan-approval-2030/\\n\\nBeInCrypto said Bloomberg's sourcing indicated Polymarket had appointed Mike Eidlin to lead the Japan effort. The report said Eidlin currently heads Japan operations at crypto firm Jupiter and that Polymarket views Japan as a large untapped opportunity. BeInCrypto added an important operational detail: Japan currently sits on Polymarket's frontend-restricted list, meaning the country is not simply a potential growth market but one where the company would need formal regulatory progress before broad access can be opened. The article said the four-year timeline gives Polymarket room to court Tokyo regulators and argued that the platform is leaning into new markets as U.S. scrutiny tightens and other governments close access. BeInCrypto also supplied additional international context, noting that Argentina ordered a nationwide Polymarket block in March and that the platform already restricts or blocks access in more than 30 countries, including France, Germany, Italy, Australia, and Poland. It further said that Polymarket had been barred from the U.S. for roughly three years before regaining CFTC clearance in September 2025, highlighting that the company is now trying to build legitimate regulated entry rather than relying on offshore availability.\\n\\nSOURCE: Crypto Briefing\\nURL: https://cryptobriefing.com/polymarket-japan-prediction-market-approval/\\n\\nCrypto Briefing framed the move as Polymarket's most ambitious international play yet. It said the New York-based platform is actively preparing to secure regulatory approval in Japan by 2030 and stressed that appointing a local representative makes the effort look more serious than a vague expansion aspiration. The article argued that Japan combines a strong speculative trading culture with rigid gambling laws, creating both a large addressable market and a difficult regulatory hurdle. Crypto Briefing added a key analytical point: the lobbying effort is likely to pitch prediction markets not as gambling products but as information-aggregation tools. That distinction could be crucial for Japanese authorities deciding whether event contracts belong under gambling restrictions, financial rules, or some novel framework. The piece also said that if Polymarket succeeds in Japan, it would represent one of the strongest precedents yet for regulated prediction markets in a major advanced economy, potentially influencing regulatory conversations across Asia. It noted that the long runway itself is revealing, because four years suggests management expects a complicated policy process rather than a near-term approval.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nAcross the three sources, the core event is clear: Polymarket is no longer just fighting piecemeal access battles but is making a deliberate strategic push to win formal approval in Japan by 2030. The story matters because it sits at the intersection of crypto regulation, prediction markets, and geographic expansion into one of the world's largest and most tightly governed financial and speculative markets. The repeated appearance of Mike Eidlin as the local lead indicates this is a concrete lobbying and market-entry effort, not just a rumor about passive interest. The recurring emphasis on Japan's strict gambling code, cautious crypto regulation, and Polymarket's current frontend restriction shows the challenge is structural. At the same time, the shared reporting that Polymarket sees meaningful organic demand in Japan suggests the company believes there is enough latent user interest to justify a multiyear campaign. The broader significance is that success in Japan would give Polymarket a template for operating within formal rules in hard jurisdictions after years of facing blocks, restrictions, and legal scrutiny elsewhere. In that sense, the news is not just about Japan; it is about whether crypto-native prediction markets can evolve from regulatory edge cases into licensed products with a recognized role in mainstream financial and information markets. That makes the Japan push one of the most consequential single-topic crypto policy stories moving across multiple feeds today.\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523460549}}
{"type":"message","id":"019746e0","parentId":"7481588e","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:20.550Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bLVIgF9VuitBHhUwL4dejYqf","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"polymarket-polygon-exploit-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Polymarket rewards-system exploit on Polygon drains over $520,000 while core user funds remain safe\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Polymarket exploit\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/22/zachxbt-flags-usd520k-polymarket-exploit-on-polygon-team-says-funds-are-safe\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/polymarket-exploit-with-520000-in-losses/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Blockchain investigator ZachXBT said more than $520,000 was drained from two Polymarket-related smart contracts on Polygon.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported the affected contract addresses as 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\",\n    \"Polymarket developers said the incident was a private-key compromise tied to an internal operations wallet used for rewards payouts, not a broader failure of core contracts or market resolution systems.\",\n    \"Polygon Labs CTO Mudit Gupta said Polymarket contracts are safe, user funds are safe, and the compromised component appeared to be the market initializer rather than the contracts themselves.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto identified the affected component as Polymarket’s UMA CTF Adapter, which connects prediction-market settlement to UMA’s Optimistic Oracle.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto, citing Bubblemaps, said the exploiter dispersed the stolen proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern often associated with early-stage laundering attempts.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto added broader context that May had already seen five hacks in the prior week, 19 incidents for the month, and roughly $38.2 million in cumulative exploit losses according to DeFiLlama data.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"# ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe\\n\\n## Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world’s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\n#### What to know:\\n\\n- Blockchain sleuth ZachXBT flagged a suspected security breach at Polymarket in which more than $520,000 was drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain.\\n- Polymarket developers said the incident stemmed from a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet tied to its rewards payout system, stressing that user funds and market resolutions remain safe.\\n\\nBlockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world’s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n\\nOver $520,000 was reportedly drained from two smart contracts on the Polygon blockchain, according to on-chain data shared by ZachXBT. The affected addresses are 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5, with funds allegedly sent to attacker address 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nPolymarket developers said the company is aware of reports tied to its rewards payout system in a post on X. The team emphasized that user funds and market resolutions remain safe, describing the issue as a private key compromise of an internal operations wallet rather than a broader smart contract exploit or core infrastructure breach. Further updates are expected.\\n\\nPolygon Labs' CTO Mudit Gupta also commented on the incident, stating:\\n\\n“Polymarket contracts are safe. User funds are safe. Looks like their market initializer was compromised. No impact to the users or the contracts.”\\n\\nPolymarket has not yet issued an official statement from its main X account. CoinDesk has reached out to the company for additional comment. The incident comes amid heightened scrutiny of decentralized finance platforms.\\n\\n**On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket’s UMA CTF Adapter.**\\n\\nThe UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket’s prediction markets settle using UMA’s Optimistic Oracle\\n\\nAccording to the alert, attackers have drained more than $520,000 so far. ZachXBT highlighted that the attacker’s address was 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n\\nAccording to Bubblemaps, the exploiter has since dispersed the proceeds across 15 addresses. This pattern is typically seen in the early stages of an on-chain laundering attempt.\\n\\n*Follow us on X** to get the latest news as it happens*\\n\\nThe situation is developing, and the full scope of the incident, as well as any response from Polymarket, has yet to be confirmed. BeInCrypto has contacted Polymarket for comment on the reported exploit and will update this story as more information becomes available.\\n\\nThe Polymarket incident lands amid a broader spike in DeFi exploits this month. May has already produced five separate hacks in the past week alone, pushing the monthly tally to 19, according to DeFiLlama data. Cumulative losses across those incidents have reached roughly $38.2 million.\\n\\n*Subscribe to our YouTube** channel to watch leaders and journalists provide expert insights*\\n\\nFeed metadata and corroborating details:\\n- CoinDesk RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T09:39:55+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT flags $520K Polymarket exploit on Polygon, team says funds are safe. Summary: Blockchain investigator ZachXBT has highlighted a suspected security breach involving Polymarket, the world’s largest decentralized prediction market platform.\\n- BeInCrypto RSS publication time: 2026-05-22T08:32:43+00:00. Headline: ZachXBT Flags Polymarket Smart-Contract Exploit With $520,000 in Losses. Summary: On-chain investigator ZachXBT issued a community alert flagging an apparent exploit of Polymarket’s UMA CTF Adapter. The UMA CTF Adapter is the smart contract that lets Polymarket’s prediction markets settle using UMA’s Optimistic Oracle.\\n- CoinDesk article references two affected addresses: 0x871D7c0f9E19001fC01E04e6cdFa7fA20f929082 and 0x91430CaD2d3975766499717fA0D66A78D814E5c5. The alleged attacker address is 0x8F98075db5d6C620e8D420A8c516E2F2059d9B91.\\n- BeInCrypto reports Bubblemaps said the exploiter dispersed proceeds across 15 addresses, a pattern it described as typical of an early-stage laundering attempt.\\n- CoinDesk embedded/linked X posts: https://x.com/PolymarketDevs/status/2057755280641814769 and https://x.com/Mudit__Gupta/status/2057749582575202570.\\n- URL validation results before inclusion: CoinDesk 200 OK; BeInCrypto returned 403 to direct curl, but its RSS entry and trafilatura extraction still produced article text, title-aligned facts, and publication timestamp.\\n\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523460549}}
{"type":"message","id":"26c5b6dd","parentId":"019746e0","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:20.550Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_r8YMCULgC5ZNSs24viTNcw9B","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"us-arma-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US lawmakers move to codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve through the American Reserve Modernization Act\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Strategic Bitcoin reserve\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US lawmakers push ARMA bill to lock a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"CryptoSlate\",\n    \"CryptoFox\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\",\n    \"https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\",\n    \"https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Representative Nick Begich introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act, with Democrat Jared Golden serving as the primary co-lead, to put a US strategic Bitcoin reserve into statute rather than leave it to executive discretion.\",\n    \"The proposal would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a Bitcoin reserve for at least 20 years, while also establishing a separate Digital Asset Stockpile for federally held non-Bitcoin crypto assets.\",\n    \"Cross-referenced reporting says the reserve would consolidate crypto already held across federal agencies, much of it originating from seizures, forfeitures and penalties.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph and CryptoSlate both reported the US currently holds about 328,372 BTC, worth roughly $25 billion to $25.5 billion, making it the largest nation-state Bitcoin holder.\",\n    \"The bill includes transparency measures such as public proof-of-reserve reporting, independent third-party audits and direct congressional oversight of the reserve.\",\n    \"Coverage also highlights a budget-neutral growth concept for the reserve, while explicitly protecting Americans’ lawful rights to own, transfer and self-custody digital assets.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"\\n\\nSOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\\n\\n#### In brief\\n\\n- Reps. Nick Begich (R-AK) and Jared Golden (D-ME) introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act.\\n- The bill seeks formally codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, which would be managed for at least 20 years.\\n- Aimed at cementing a core Trump administration promise, the initiative has faced ongoing hurdles.\\n\\nRep. Nick Begich (R-AK) introduced legislation on Thursday aimed at enshrining a strategic reserve for Bitcoin in federal law, seeking to cement one of President Donald Trump’s core campaign promises for digital asset holders ahead of the high-stakes U.S. midterm elections.\\n\\nThe American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a reserve of the digital asset for a minimum of 20 years, while establishing a stockpile for other cryptocurrencies held by the government.\\n\\nThe legislation also mandates the consolidation of what is believed to be billions of dollars in digital assets held across federal agencies—attained through actions like forfeitures and penalties—while providing transparency through a proof-of-reserve report.\\n\\nAs of last month, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin that Trump created via executive order is still a work in progress. At a conference in Las Vegas, Patrick Witt, executive director of the President’s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, said the current administration has spent months “figuring out” the legal interpretations associated with the initiative.\\n\\nAmerica’s reserves balance sheet is a critical component of our nation’s insurance policy, bolstering our currency and providing assurance during times of uncertainty. Over time, the prevailing sentiment as to what constitutes a durable store of value can shift, and as such it is… pic.twitter.com/7XfBwAWFGi\\n\\n— Congressman Nick Begich (@RepNickBegich) May 21, 2026\\n\\nJoining Begich as the bill’s primary Democratic co-lead is Rep. Jared Golden (D-ME). The ARMA Act meanwhile counts 17 original co-sponsors, including Rep. Mike Collins (R-GA), among the first lawmakers to disclose an investment in a meme coin on Capitol Hill.\\n\\n“Administrations have auctioned [crypto] off or held it in reserve, according to the whims of the executive branch,” Golden said in a statement, noting that a stockpile created under the ARMA Act would enjoy the “weight of law.”\\n\\nAlthough a former administration official had said that the White House was eager to purchase as much Bitcoin as possible in a “budget-neutral way,” Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent later dented enthusiasm by ruling out any agency purchases.\\n\\nThe bill is aimed at providing the flexibility needed to broaden America’s portfolio of reserve assets that effectively serve as insurance policy, while also safeguarding crypto from “the whims of Congress or future administrations,” Begich said in an X post on Thursday.\\n\\nEstablished in May 2025 with a stroke of the president’s pen, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin was swiftly skewered by critics, including Rep. Maxine Waters (D-CA), the ranking member of the House Financial Services Committee, who warned of benefits for Trump insiders.\\n\\n“Typically, when the government holds strategic reserves, it is for an essential input that powers the U.S. economy and day-to-day life for American families,” she said. “Crypto, however, does not fall into these categories, because it has no inherent value.”\\n\\nAlthough the much-hyped stockpile’s creation represented a symbolic win for the digital assets industry, the government has yet to announce any allocations. Last year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis (R-WY) re-introduced the BITCOIN Act, a bill that directs the Treasury to purchase 1 million Bitcoin over five years in a way that seeks to avoid taxing citizens.\\n\\n*Decrypt* has reached out to Begich and Golden for comment.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinTelegraph\\nURL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\\n\\nIn an interview on Sunday, Patrick Witt, of the President’s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, referred to ARMA as “Version 2” of the BITCOIN Act and said the White House has spent considerable time examining the legal implications of a Bitcoin reserve.\\n\\n“It's a breakthrough as far as getting everything in place, legally sound, properly safeguarding the assets.”\\n\\nThe push for a federal policy comes as the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin worth more than $25.5 billion — the most of any nation-state — but has sold portions of those holdings through court-ordered actions over the years.\\n\\n“The US is already one of the largest holders of Bitcoin in the world. But Congress has never set a federal policy on what to do with that asset,” said US Representative Jared Golden, one of the 16 co-sponsors of the bill.\\n\\nUnder ARMA, Bitcoin must be held for a minimum of 20 years unless it is sold to reduce America’s national debt, which topped $39 trillion on Wednesday.\\n\\nLike the BITCOIN Act, ARMA also seeks to acquire up to 1 million Bitcoin over five years through budget-neutral strategies, meaning it would avoid using taxpayer money.\\n\\nUS Representative Mike Carey argued that as digital assets continue to grow in importance, the bill could strengthen America’s long-term economic position and help keep it “competitive on the world stage.”\\n\\n**Related: ****SpaceX reveals larger-than-expected Bitcoin holdings in IPO filing**** **\\n\\nStrive CEO and chairman Matt Cole said ARMA is the “single most important crypto legislation” that could come out of Washington DC.\\n\\nQuarterly proof of reserve reports and independent third-party audits of the Bitcoin reserve would be published under ARMA, Begich noted.\\n\\nThe bill also seeks to protect digital property rights by affirming that the federal government may not impair the right of individuals to own or self-custody digital assets.\\n\\n**Magazine: ****eToro founder timed Bitcoin top perfectly due to belief in 4 year cycles**\\n\\nMore on the subject\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CryptoSlate\\nURL: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\\n\\n# US lawmakers push new Strategic Bitcoin Reserve act to secure $25 billion federal stash\\n\\nARMA would force the government to hold its Bitcoin for two decades to build a strategic financial buffer.\\n\\nA renewed push to solidify the United States’ foothold in the digital asset market emerged Thursday as lawmakers unveiled legislation to establish a formal strategic Bitcoin reserve and consolidate the federal government's multibillion-dollar cryptocurrency stockpile.\\n\\nSpearheaded by Representative Nick Begich, the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) of 2026 mandates the creation of a secure Bitcoin reserve within the US Department of the Treasury, alongside a separate “Digital Asset Stockpile” for federally held, non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies.\\n\\nThe legislation aims to provide a long-term structure to the government’s existing digital wealth, primarily amassed through law enforcement seizures. According to data from Bitcoin Treasuries, the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin, a stash valued at more than $25 billion.\\n\\nBegich said in a statement:\\n\\n“The American Reserve Modernization Act positions the United States to lead confidently in the digital age while protecting taxpayer interests, strengthening financial sovereignty, and reinforcing the principles of transparency and sound stewardship.”\\n\\nThe bill has 17 original House co-sponsors, including Buddy Carter, Ben Cline of Virginia, Barry Moore of Alabama, Burgess Owens of Utah, Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa, Mike Carey and Michael Rulli of Ohio, Mike Collins of Georgia, Mike Lawler of New York, Riley Moore of West Virginia, and Tim Moore of North Carolina.\\n\\nApart from the strong legislative support, crypto industry leaders are already throwing their weight behind the measure.\\n\\nMatt Cole, CEO of Bitcoin treasury firm Strive, called the proposal “the single most important crypto legislation that can come out of D.C.” for the long-term health and security of the United States.\\n\\n## ARMA establishes a 20-year Bitcoin bet\\n\\nIf enacted, the bill would radically alter how Washington handles digital assets. Rather than periodically auctioning off seized crypto, the Treasury would be forced to consolidate custody and oversight across all federal agencies.\\n\\nCrucially, the legislation requires that any Bitcoin held in the strategic reserve be maintained for at least 20 years, effectively turning the US government into a long-term “hodler.”\\n\\nTo address persistent industry demands for transparency, the bill establishes stringent reporting measures. The Treasury would be required to publish quarterly public “Proof of Reserve” reports, submit to independent third-party audits, and face direct congressional oversight.\\n\\nThe legislation also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies. The goal is to evaluate lawful methods for the government to expand its digital reserves without relying on tax increases, deficit spending, or adding to the national debt.\\n\\nFurthermore, the bill offers an olive branch to crypto advocates by explicitly protecting self-custody rights, affirming that the federal government may not impair Americans' lawful right to own, transfer, or independently secure their digital assets.\\n\\n## Mounting\n\n⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]\n\n structure that would make the proposal a direct bet on converting part of the federal balance sheet into Bitcoin exposure.\\n\\nLummis legislation followed President Donald Trump's executive order to establish a strategic Bitcoin reserve.\\n\\nHowever, these initiatives have considerably stalled despite the Trump administration's pro-crypto stance.\\n\\nStill, the Begich bill aims to build on the recent advancement of the Clarity Act, which passed the Senate Banking Committee with bipartisan support. That bill is designed to establish clear rules of the road for the industry and encourage institutional investment.\\n\\nBitcoin is **-0.82%** over the past 24 hours and currently sits at rank #1 by market cap.\\n\\n## Where the broader market sits right now\\n\\nRight now, the total crypto market is valued at **$2.59T** with **$76.67B** in 24-hour volume. Bitcoin dominance sits at **59.97%**. Explore the market\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CryptoFox\\nURL: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\\n\\nUS Representative **Nick Begich** has introduced the **Reserve Modernization Act**, a legislative proposal designed to formalize the role of digital assets within the national treasury. Supported by **16 original co-sponsors**, the bill seeks to establish a permanent **strategic Bitcoin reserve**, integrating federally held digital assets into a long-term storage framework. This initiative marks a significant shift in federal fiscal policy, aiming to treat **BTC** as a cornerstone of the nation's financial security for the coming decades.\\n\\n## Legislative Framework and 20-Year Lock-In Period\\n\\nThe proposed act focuses on the long-term preservation of digital assets. Under the terms of the legislation, any Bitcoin held within the strategic reserve would be **locked for a minimum period of 20 years**. This provision is intended to prevent short-term liquidation and ensure that the asset serves as a generational store of value. *By mandating a two-decade holding period, the bill aims to insulate the reserve from market volatility and shifting political priorities.*\\n\\nThe bill also outlines strategies for the **integration of existing federally held digital assets**, which often originate from law enforcement seizures. Rather than auctioning these assets, the government would transition them into the sovereign reserve.\\n\\n## Budget Neutrality and Legal Protections\\n\\nA key component of the Reserve Modernization Act is its focus on **budget-neutral acquisition strategies**. Lawmakers are exploring methods to expand the Bitcoin holdings without increasing the national deficit or requiring new taxpayer funding. Furthermore, the bill includes specific legal safeguards to:\\n\\n- Protect the reserve from\\n**future liquidation efforts**by subsequent administrations. - Establish clear jurisdictional oversight for the management of the\\n**blockchain-based assets**. - Codify the reserve into\\n**official law**, providing a more permanent foundation than previous executive orders.\\n\\n*While prior administrative actions had moved toward creating a reserve through executive authority, this legislative approach seeks to provide a statutory mandate that would be significantly harder to reverse.*\\n\\nIn conclusion, the introduction of the Reserve Modernization Act on **May 22, 2026**, represents a major step toward the institutionalization of cryptocurrency at the federal level. By proposing a mandatory holding period and budget-neutral growth, the bill aims to stabilize the national balance sheet through the inclusion of decentralized assets. The progress of this legislation will be closely monitored by global markets as an indicator of the United States' long-term stance on **digital finance** and sovereign asset management.\\n\\n## Frequently Asked *Questions*\\n\\nQuick answers to the most common questions about this topic.\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}[... 418 more characters truncated]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523460550}}
{"type":"message","id":"0f110080","parentId":"26c5b6dd","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:04:53.162Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e6mCxBesDyVB9nqCtrFmQfxd","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, textwrap, os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nout = {\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"congress-polymarket-kalshi-insider-trading-probe-2026-05-23\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US congressional insider-trading probe into prediction markets Polymarket and Kalshi\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"prediction market probe\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Congress opens insider-trading probe into Polymarket and Kalshi over fears government officials could profit from nonpublic information\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer said the committee is investigating Polymarket and Kalshi over whether government employees may be using insider or classified information to profit from prediction-market trades tied to policy, elections, wars, and other state actions.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour seeking records on identity verification, geographic restrictions, and how the platforms detect and flag anomalous trading activity.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Comer referenced suspiciously timed trades highlighted in a May 13 New York Times report, including bets linked to Israel's military actions against Iran, a Trump ceasefire announcement, and congressional election contracts.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph added that prosecutors previously charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke with using confidential government information to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket contracts tied to the capture of Nicolás Maduro; Van Dyke pleaded not guilty.\",\n    \"CoinDesk cited Bubblemaps CEO Nicolas Vaiman saying his team identified 80 Polymarket bets with a 98% win rate, a pattern he argued is statistically impossible to explain by luck and therefore suggestive of information asymmetry or insider trading.\",\n    \"Decrypt reported that Kalshi launched Americans for Fair Markets, a well-capitalized advocacy group tied to former White House communications aide Taylor Budowich, on the same day the probe became public, underscoring how high the policy stakes have become for prediction markets in Washington.\",\n    \"CoinDesk said Bernstein estimated prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could hit about $240 billion in 2026, and may grow toward $1 trillion by 2030, explaining why lawmakers now see the sector as large enough to create national-security, ethics, and consumer-protection risks.\",\n    \"Across the coverage, both companies said they maintain anti-insider-trading safeguards and will engage with lawmakers, while the probe could lead to legislation restricting or banning participation by members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": textwrap.dedent(\"\"\"\n    SOURCE: CoinDesk\n    URL: https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\n\n    CoinDesk reported that the U.S. House Oversight Committee is opening a probe into prediction-market platforms Polymarket and Kalshi amid fears that government employees could use insider or classified information to make money on contracts tied to public policy, elections, and geopolitical events. The article said Chair James Comer told CNBC that members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees may have access to information that could generate “huge profits” when traded through prediction markets. CoinDesk said Comer plans not only to investigate how widespread such conduct may already be, but also to build a case for legislation that could bar government officials from participating in these markets entirely.\n\n    The piece said Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour requesting internal records and clarity on a set of core compliance controls. Those requests focus on identity verification, enforcement of geographic restrictions, and the detection of anomalous trading activity. CoinDesk framed the probe as part of a broader congressional effort to understand whether these platforms can be manipulated by actors with privileged access to national-security or policy information.\n\n    CoinDesk also gave important market context. It cited a Bernstein report from April estimating that prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could rise to around $240 billion in 2026, and may approach $1 trillion by 2030 as the category expands from niche wagering into broader “information markets” spanning sports, politics, crypto, and macroeconomic events. The article further quoted Bubblemaps co-founder and CEO Nicolas Vaiman, who warned that visible irregular betting patterns could create national-security concerns if hostile foreign actors can infer privileged knowledge from public market activity. Vaiman said his team found 80 bets on Polymarket with a 98% win rate, arguing that such results are statistically implausible through luck alone.\n\n    SOURCE: CoinTelegraph\n    URL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n\n    CoinTelegraph confirmed that the investigation centers on allegedly suspiciously timed trades involving government-sensitive events. The outlet said Comer was referring in part to a May 13 New York Times report describing users who bet on Israel’s military actions against Iran, on President Donald Trump announcing a ceasefire in that conflict, and on contracts connected to congressional elections. According to CoinTelegraph, Comer’s public comments suggest lawmakers believe prediction markets may offer a new venue where politically connected or government-linked insiders can monetize nonpublic information faster than in traditional financial markets.\n\n    CoinTelegraph added legally significant background by pointing to a prior Justice Department case announced in April. Prosecutors charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke over allegations that he used confidential government information related to the operation targeting Nicolás Maduro to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket event contracts. The report said Van Dyke pleaded not guilty and was released on $250,000 bail with travel restrictions. That case matters because it gives Congress a concrete enforcement example rather than a purely theoretical risk.\n\n    The article also noted that Polymarket said in March it had updated its approach to potential insider trading, while Kalshi said in April that it had barred three U.S. politicians from betting on their own races. Both firms indicated they would engage with lawmakers and defended their integrity controls. CoinTelegraph’s framing makes clear that the present probe is not happening in isolation: it follows a week of intensifying scrutiny in Washington and escalating concern that event contracts may blur the boundary between financial markets, gambling, and ethics law.\n\n    SOURCE: Decrypt\n    URL: https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\n\n    Decrypt approached the story through Kalshi’s policy response. It reported that Kalshi unveiled Americans for Fair Markets, described as a “well-capitalized” advocacy group, the same day Congress opened the insider-trading probe. The group tapped Taylor Budowich, a former White House communications official and pro-Trump PAC leader, as a strategic adviser. Decrypt said the launch is designed to shape how Capitol Hill views prediction markets and to counter attacks from entrenched sportsbook and casino interests.\n\n    Decrypt stressed that Kalshi is trying to distinguish itself from Polymarket and from traditional betting operators by emphasizing its status as a federally regulated exchange under the CFTC. The article quoted rhetoric from Americans for Fair Markets arguing that banning prediction markets would simply push activity offshore to venues with weaker identity checks, lighter consumer protections, and less enforcement against insider trading. At the same time, Decrypt showed that the gaming lobby is fighting back, with American Gaming Association CEO Bill Miller telling Congress that these platforms are essentially backdoor sports-betting operations wrapped in financial-market language.\n\n    COMBINED SYNTHESIS\n\n    Across all three reports, the strongest single event is not merely that Congress asked a few questions, but that U.S. lawmakers have escalated prediction markets into a national policy issue touching ethics, market integrity, gambling law, and national security. The probe matters because Polymarket and Kalshi sit at the center of a rapidly expanding sector whose contracts can reflect military action, diplomatic announcements, elections, and government decisions in real time. If officials with privileged access are trading in that environment, the issue is larger than ordinary platform compliance; it becomes a state-integrity problem.\n\n    The coverage also reveals why this story has momentum right now. CoinDesk provides scale, showing prediction markets are becoming large enough to matter systemically. CoinTelegraph provides the enforcement trail and the concrete Maduro-related case that makes the risk legible to lawmakers. Decrypt shows the immediate political counteroffensive, with Kalshi moving to build a lobbying apparatus as scrutiny intensifies. Taken together, the reporting suggests Washington is entering a new phase: prediction markets are no longer just an innovation story or a crypto-curiosity story, but a contested regulatory battleground that could soon produce new rules on who may trade, what event contracts are permissible, and how platforms must police insider information.\n  \"\"\").strip()\n}\n\ntext = json.dumps(out, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\nfor path in [\"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/raw.json\", \"/tmp/researcher-raw.txt\"]:\n    Path(path).write_text(text)\nprint('wrote', len(text), 'bytes')\nPY","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":7008,"output":2734,"cacheRead":24320,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":34062,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779523460552,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DibdGUgy5GdEFkZPUFdJWMERKXzlF"}}
